"""~100-word UK precis for the new UK platforms (shown on platform detail pages).
Generated via Claude (claude-sonnet-4-6), British English + GBP. Keyed by platform name."""

LONG_DESCRIPTIONS = {
    "YunoJuno": "YunoJuno is a leading UK freelance marketplace that connects independent professionals — from designers and developers to marketers and strategists — with major British brands, agencies, and businesses. Freelancers create a profile, showcase their portfolio, and apply for roles posted by vetted clients. You earn by securing project-based or contract work, with YunoJuno handling invoicing and payments. It is particularly well-regarded in the creative and digital industries. As a freelancer, you are self-employed and responsible for reporting income to HMRC. IR35 considerations may apply depending on how engagements are structured, so understanding your tax position is advisable before taking on work.",
    "Malt": "Malt is a European freelance marketplace with a growing UK presence, designed to connect skilled independent professionals directly with companies seeking project-based expertise. Freelancers build a detailed profile highlighting their skills, day rate, and experience, then respond to client requests or appear in search results. Earnings come from agreed project fees, with Malt taking a service commission. It suits experienced UK freelancers in tech, marketing, consulting, and creative fields who prefer direct client relationships without a middleman agency. As a self-employed individual, you must declare all income to HMRC and submit a Self Assessment tax return. Rates and opportunities vary by specialism and demand.",
    "Worksome": "Worksome is a UK-focused freelance and contractor platform that simplifies the process of finding and completing contract work by managing contracts, compliance, and payments in one place. It is particularly notable for its attention to IR35 status determinations, helping both clients and contractors navigate the UK's off-payroll working rules with greater confidence. Freelancers create a profile and are matched with relevant opportunities across sectors including technology, finance, and operations. Earnings are paid promptly once work is completed. As with any self-employed arrangement, UK contractors remain responsible for their own tax affairs and should consult a professional adviser regarding their IR35 status where appropriate.",
    "Bark": "Bark is a UK-based leads marketplace that connects local professionals with customers searching for services across hundreds of categories, from photography and tutoring to plumbing and legal advice. Rather than applying for jobs directly, professionals purchase credits to respond to relevant customer enquiries in their area. Earnings depend on successfully converting those leads into paying clients, so there is an upfront cost before income is guaranteed. It suits self-employed tradespeople, creatives, and service providers looking to grow a local client base. All income earned must be declared to HMRC, and as with any self-employment, results will vary depending on your profession, location, and responsiveness.",
    "Codementor": "Codementor is an online platform where experienced developers and programmers can earn money by mentoring less experienced coders or taking on freelance technical projects for clients worldwide. UK-based users can set their own hourly rate, offer one-to-one sessions, answer questions, or complete longer development projects. Payments are processed via the platform and can be transferred to UK bank accounts or PayPal. It suits developers who enjoy teaching as well as building, and is particularly useful for those looking to supplement primary income. All earnings are self-employment income and must be reported to HMRC via Self Assessment. Income will vary based on expertise, availability, and demand.",
    "Designhill": "Designhill is an international creative marketplace that allows UK-based graphic designers to earn money by participating in design contests or completing direct freelance projects covering logos, branding, websites, and more. In contests, multiple designers submit concepts and only the winning entry receives payment, making income less predictable than fixed-price project work. Direct projects offer a more reliable earning route once a reputation is established. Designhill suits designers comfortable working speculatively and keen to build an international portfolio. Payments can be withdrawn to PayPal or bank accounts accessible from the UK. Income earned through the platform is self-employment income and must be declared to HMRC accordingly.",
    "Stuart": "Stuart is an on-demand logistics platform operating in major UK cities, connecting couriers with businesses that need fast, reliable deliveries of food, parcels, and retail goods. You can work using a bicycle, scooter, or car, choosing jobs that match your vehicle type and availability through the Stuart app. Like all gig-economy platforms, you work on a self-employed basis, meaning you must register with HMRC, file a Self Assessment tax return, and organise your own insurance. Stuart suits couriers who want variety in their deliveries rather than a single-employer arrangement. Pay per job and total earnings will vary depending on city, order volume, and hours worked.",
    "Gophr": "Gophr is a UK-based same-day courier platform that connects self-employed couriers with businesses and individuals needing urgent deliveries across cities. Unlike some platforms, Gophr allows you to select jobs that suit your specific vehicle, whether that is a bicycle, motorcycle, van, or car, giving you greater control over the work you take on. Earnings are job-based and will fluctuate depending on your location, availability, and the types of deliveries you accept. As a self-employed worker, you are responsible for registering with HMRC, paying your own tax and National Insurance, and holding appropriate vehicle and courier insurance. Gophr can suit couriers who prefer flexibility and varied delivery work.",
    "Evri Courier": "Evri, formerly known as Hermes, is one of the UK's largest parcel-delivery networks, and its self-employed courier model allows individuals to take on a local delivery round in their area. Rather than responding to on-demand jobs, Evri couriers typically work an established route, delivering parcels to homes and businesses in a defined area. Pay is generally calculated per parcel delivered, so your earnings will depend on the volume of parcels on your round and how efficiently you complete deliveries. As a self-employed contractor, you must register with HMRC and manage your own tax and National Insurance obligations. A suitable vehicle and the ability to handle physical work are essential.",
    "Bolt": "Bolt is a ride-hailing platform operating in London and a growing number of other UK cities, offering drivers the opportunity to earn by transporting passengers through its app. Bolt positions itself as a lower-commission alternative to some rivals, meaning drivers may retain a higher proportion of each fare. To drive on the platform in the UK, you must hold a valid private-hire vehicle licence issued by a relevant local authority, such as TfL in London. As a self-employed driver, you are responsible for reporting your income to HMRC via Self Assessment. Earnings will vary based on your city, the hours you drive, and passenger demand at any given time.",
    "Addison Lee": "Addison Lee is one of London's most established private-hire operators, with a strong reputation among both corporate and personal clients. Drivers can work with Addison Lee to transport passengers across the capital, benefiting from a recognisable brand that can attract a steady stream of bookings. To join, you will need a valid TfL private-hire vehicle licence and a suitable, approved vehicle. Like other private-hire work, you operate on a self-employed basis, so registering with HMRC and managing your own tax and National Insurance is essential. Addison Lee may suit experienced drivers who prefer working within a professional, well-organised fleet rather than a purely app-based, open-marketplace environment.",
    "Airtasker": "Airtasker is an online marketplace that connects people who need tasks completed with local 'Taskers' who are willing to do the work for an agreed fee. Jobs posted on the platform range from furniture assembly, cleaning, and handyman repairs to removals, gardening, and office admin, making it suitable for people with a broad variety of practical or professional skills. You browse available tasks, submit an offer, and negotiate directly with the person posting the job. Airtasker charges a service fee on completed tasks. As earnings come from self-employment, you must register with HMRC and declare your income accordingly. Actual earnings depend entirely on the tasks you secure and the rates you negotiate.",
    "Bidvine": "Bidvine is a UK-based services marketplace that connects skilled professionals with local customers searching for tradespeople, tutors, fitness instructors, photographers and more. You create a free profile, receive matched job requests from nearby customers and submit quotes to win work. It suits anyone with a marketable trade or skill who wants a steady flow of local leads without heavy marketing effort. Because you are working for yourself, earnings count as self-employment income and must be declared to HMRC via a Self Assessment tax return. Income will vary depending on your profession, location and the effort you put into winning quotes.",
    "BorrowMyDoggy": "BorrowMyDoggy is a popular UK platform that connects dog owners with local borrowers who are happy to walk, sit or care for their pets. Dog lovers pay a small annual membership fee to join, after which they can connect with owners in their area and agree arrangements directly. Earnings potential is modest and often supplemented by the simple joy of spending time with dogs, making it ideal as a side hustle rather than a primary income. Any money received for dog-care services is technically taxable income and should be declared to HMRC if it exceeds your personal tax-free trading allowance.",
    "Appen": "Appen is a global technology company that offers flexible, work-from-home project opportunities to UK residents, including data annotation, search engine evaluation and AI training tasks. You apply for individual projects, complete online assessments to qualify and then work independently within set weekly hour limits. It suits people who are detail-oriented and comfortable working online without close supervision. Pay rates and available projects vary, and work can be inconsistent between contracts. As an independent contractor, you are responsible for reporting your earnings to HMRC, and income received should be declared if it exceeds your tax-free trading allowance.",
    "TELUS International AI": "TELUS International AI, formerly known as Lionbridge AI, is a remote working platform offering UK residents flexible opportunities as search raters, data annotators and AI project contributors. Tasks typically involve reviewing web content, rating search results for relevance and quality, or completing language-based data projects. Work is project-based and hours can vary considerably, making it better suited as a supplementary income rather than a reliable full-time role. You work as an independent contractor, so earnings must be declared to HMRC as self-employment income. No specialist qualifications are usually required, though strong attention to detail and good English language skills are essential.",
    "Premise": "Premise is a smartphone app available to UK users that pays you to complete short, location-based data-collection tasks in your local area. Tasks can include photographing shop fronts, checking product prices, verifying business information or answering survey questions while you are out and about. Rewards are paid in points that can be redeemed for cash or vouchers, though earning potential per task tends to be modest. It works best as a way to earn a little extra while running everyday errands rather than as a meaningful income source. Earnings should be considered alongside your personal tax-free trading allowance and declared to HMRC where applicable.",
    "Streetbees": "Streetbees is a UK market-research app that pays users to complete short missions involving surveys, photos, videos and diary-style accounts of everyday experiences such as shopping trips or meal times. The platform works with major brands and research agencies to gather authentic consumer insights. Missions are delivered via a chat-style interface, making them feel conversational and straightforward to complete. Rewards are paid directly to your PayPal account, though earnings per mission are generally small and availability can be inconsistent. Streetbees suits people who enjoy sharing opinions in their spare time. Any income earned should be declared to HMRC if it exceeds the tax-free trading allowance.",
    "Roamler": "Roamler is a UK mystery-shopping and retail-auditing platform that pays smartphone users to complete in-store tasks at supermarkets, shops and retail outlets across the country. Typical tasks include checking shelf availability, verifying promotional displays, photographing products or completing short in-store audits. You choose tasks near you via the app, complete them to the required standard and receive payment upon approval. It suits people who are frequently out and about in retail environments and want to earn a little extra alongside daily activities. Earnings are variable and task availability depends on your location. Income should be declared to HMRC if it exceeds your personal tax-free trading allowance.",
    "TopCashback": "TopCashback is one of the UK's most established cashback websites, allowing members to earn money back when shopping online with hundreds of retailers, from supermarkets and travel providers to insurance and utility companies. You access retailers through the TopCashback site or app, and a percentage of your spend is returned to your account as cashback, which can be withdrawn to your bank account or PayPal. Additional bonuses are available for referring friends. It suits anyone looking to reduce everyday spending rather than generate significant income. Cashback received is generally not considered taxable income for most users, but if you earn referral bonuses regularly, it is worth checking your position with HMRC.",
    "Quidco": "Quidco is one of the UK's largest and most established cashback platforms, allowing members to earn money back whenever they shop online or in-store with thousands of participating retailers, from supermarkets to insurance providers. After clicking through to a retailer via Quidco and completing a qualifying purchase, a cashback amount is tracked and eventually paid into your account, withdrawable via bank transfer, PayPal, or gift card. It suits anyone looking to reduce everyday spending without changing their shopping habits. Earnings vary by retailer and offer, and cashback is not guaranteed until confirmed. Amounts earned may need to be declared to HMRC depending on your personal tax situation.",
    "JustPark": "JustPark is a well-established UK platform that lets homeowners, businesses, and landlords rent out unused driveways, garages, or parking spaces to drivers searching for affordable parking nearby. You list your space for free, set your own availability and pricing, and JustPark handles the booking and payment process, taking a commission from each transaction. It suits UK property owners with off-street parking they are not fully using, particularly those in busy urban areas, near transport hubs, stadiums, or town centres where parking demand is high. Income earned through JustPark is taxable, and regular earners should register as self-employed and report earnings to HMRC accordingly.",
    "Airbnb": "Airbnb is a globally recognised platform that allows UK residents to earn income by hosting guests in a spare room, a second property, or their entire home while they are away. You create a listing, set your own availability, house rules, and nightly price, and Airbnb handles secure payments, taking a service fee from each booking. It suits homeowners and tenants with landlord permission who want flexible, short-term letting income. UK hosts should be aware of the Rent a Room Scheme, which offers a tax allowance for letting furnished accommodation in your home, and should also check local planning rules, as some councils impose restrictions on short-term lets.",
    "Spacer": "Spacer is a UK platform that connects people who have unused storage space, such as a garage, loft, spare room, or outbuilding, with individuals and small businesses looking for affordable storage solutions near them. As a host, you list your available space for free, set your price and terms, and Spacer facilitates bookings and monthly payments, retaining a platform fee. It suits homeowners or renters with landlord and insurer permission who want to generate a steady passive income from space they are not otherwise using. Income earned is taxable, so hosts should keep clear records and report relevant earnings to HMRC, and should check their home insurance policy before listing.",
    "MyTutor": "MyTutor is the UK's leading online tutoring platform, connecting university students and graduates with pupils studying at GCSE and A-Level. Tutors deliver lessons via a purpose-built virtual classroom, making it straightforward to work from home around lectures or other commitments. You apply by submitting a short application and completing an interview, so competition can be selective. MyTutor sets the hourly rate on your behalf and pays earnings directly into your account. Because you are working as a self-employed individual, you are responsible for declaring any income to HMRC. It suits academically strong students or graduates looking for flexible, rewarding part-time work without leaving home.",
    "Tutorful": "Tutorful is a popular UK tutoring marketplace where tutors can offer one-to-one lessons online or in person across a wide range of subjects, including academic topics, music, and languages. You create a profile, set your own hourly rate, and Tutorful takes a commission on lessons booked through the platform. The site handles scheduling and payment processing, reducing admin for tutors. Because earnings are self-employed income, you must register with HMRC and report what you make. Tutorful suits tutors at any career stage — from undergraduates to qualified teachers — who want flexibility in how many hours they work and which subjects they cover.",
    "Superprof": "Superprof is a global tutoring marketplace with a strong presence in the UK, allowing tutors to advertise lessons in virtually any subject imaginable, from school curricula and languages to music, sport, and professional skills. Unlike some platforms, Superprof lets you set your own rates and keep the full amount you charge students, as the platform operates on a subscription model rather than taking commission per lesson. You manage your own bookings and communicate directly with prospective students. All earnings are self-employed income and must be declared to HMRC. It suits tutors who want maximum control over their pricing, schedule, and the subjects they teach.",
    "First Tutors": "First Tutors is one of the UK's most established tutor directories, helping tutors connect with students seeking local, in-person, or online one-to-one tuition across a broad range of subjects and levels. Tutors create a detailed profile, set their own hourly rate, and pay a membership fee to access student enquiries, after which they communicate and agree terms directly with families. Because First Tutors acts as a directory rather than a booking platform, you have full control over your schedule and pricing. All income earned is self-employed and must be declared to HMRC. It suits experienced tutors who prefer direct relationships with students and full control over their work.",
    "The Profs": "The Profs is a premium UK tutoring agency specialising in university-level, professional qualification, and admissions tutoring, typically commanding higher hourly rates than standard GCSE or A-Level platforms. Rather than self-listing, tutors apply to join the agency's vetted network and are matched with clients by The Profs team, who handle sales and client management on your behalf. The agency takes a commission from each booking in return for this service. Earnings are self-employed income and must be reported to HMRC. The Profs is best suited to postgraduate students, academics, or subject specialists with strong credentials who want access to high-value clients without having to source their own leads.",
    "Tutor House": "Tutor House is a UK tutoring platform offering one-to-one lessons at GCSE, A-Level, and degree level, with a particular concentration in London as well as growing online provision across the wider UK. Tutors apply to join the platform and, once accepted, are matched with students by the Tutor House team, which handles enquiries and scheduling support on your behalf. The platform takes a commission on lessons, and tutors are paid for confirmed sessions. As with all tutoring work, earnings are self-employed income that must be declared to HMRC. Tutor House suits qualified or highly experienced tutors, including current teachers, who want a managed service to help fill their timetable with motivated students.",
    "Freetrade": "Freetrade is a UK-based, FCA-regulated investing app that lets you buy and sell UK and US shares and ETFs without paying commission on trades. You can hold investments within a Stocks and Shares ISA to shelter potential gains from tax, or use a standard general investment account. Returns depend entirely on market performance, so your capital is at risk and there are no guaranteed profits. Freetrade suits cost-conscious UK investors who want a straightforward, mobile-first platform. Be aware that some features sit behind a paid subscription tier, and any investment income or gains outside an ISA may need to be declared to HMRC.",
    "Trading 212": "Trading 212 is an FCA-regulated, commission-free UK app for trading and investing in stocks and ETFs, and it also offers a popular cash account that has paid a competitive interest rate. You can open a Stocks and Shares ISA to keep returns sheltered from UK tax, making it appealing for both beginners and more active investors. The platform also offers CFD trading, which carries significant risk and is separate from its investing accounts. As with all investing, your capital is at risk and returns are never guaranteed. UK users should note that interest earned outside an ISA may be subject to income tax depending on personal allowances.",
    "InvestEngine": "InvestEngine is a low-cost, FCA-regulated UK platform focused exclusively on exchange-traded funds. You can build your own commission-free DIY portfolio, open a Stocks and Shares ISA, or opt for a managed portfolio where InvestEngine selects and rebalances ETFs on your behalf. It suits UK investors who want broad market exposure at minimal cost, particularly those comfortable choosing their own funds. Because returns depend on market movements, your capital is at risk and no income level is guaranteed. Gains and income held outside an ISA wrapper may be liable to capital gains tax or income tax, so it is worth keeping HMRC obligations in mind.",
    "Nutmeg": "Nutmeg is one of the UK's best-known robo-advisers and is now part of JP Morgan. It manages your money for you across a range of risk levels, offering Stocks and Shares ISAs, Lifetime ISAs, Junior ISAs, and personal pensions, so it requires very little hands-on involvement from the investor. You pay an annual management fee based on the value of your portfolio rather than per trade. Nutmeg suits busy UK savers who want professional-style portfolio management without selecting individual investments themselves. All investing carries risk and your capital can go down as well as up; any gains within an ISA wrapper are sheltered from UK tax.",
    "Moneybox": "Moneybox is a beginner-friendly UK investing app that makes saving and investing accessible by rounding up your everyday card purchases and investing the spare change into funds. Beyond the round-up feature, it offers a Stocks and Shares ISA, a Lifetime ISA (which includes a government bonus of up to £1,000 per year for eligible savers), a personal pension, and straightforward savings accounts. It is particularly well suited to first-time investors who want to build a habit gradually. Moneybox is FCA regulated, but as with all investing your capital is at risk. Returns are never guaranteed and investment income outside an ISA may be reportable to HMRC.",
    "Moneyfarm": "Moneyfarm is a UK digital wealth manager regulated by the FCA that creates and manages diversified investment portfolios on your behalf, selecting a mix of ETFs suited to your chosen risk profile. You can invest through a Stocks and Shares ISA, a personal pension, or a general investment account. The service suits UK savers who prefer a hands-off approach and want ongoing portfolio management without needing investment expertise. Fees are charged as a percentage of assets under management, so costs scale with your portfolio size. As with all investment products, your capital is at risk and past performance does not guarantee future returns; gains outside an ISA may be subject to UK tax.",
    "Wealthify": "Wealthify is a UK robo-investing service owned by Aviva, regulated by the FCA, that lets you start investing with a relatively small amount. You simply choose a risk level from cautious to adventurous, and Wealthify's team manages a diversified portfolio of funds and ETFs on your behalf. Account options include a Stocks and Shares ISA, a Junior ISA, a personal pension, and a general investment account. It is ideal for UK savers who want a straightforward, managed approach without picking their own investments. Your capital is at risk and returns are not guaranteed; income or gains held outside an ISA wrapper may need to be reported to HMRC.",
    "Plum": "Plum is a UK money app that combines smart budgeting with automated saving and investing, making it easy to grow your money without constant manual effort. It analyses your spending and automatically sets aside small amounts, which you can direct into cash savings pots or investment funds, including a Stocks and Shares ISA. Plum suits UK users who struggle to save consistently and want both financial organisation and investment growth in one app. It is FCA regulated, though investment returns depend on market performance and your capital is at risk. Some features, including access to certain funds and the ISA, require a paid subscription tier, and investment income outside an ISA may be taxable.",
    "Hargreaves Lansdown": "Hargreaves Lansdown is the UK's largest investment platform, offering private investors access to shares, funds, investment trusts, bonds, ISAs, and SIPPs all in one place. You can grow wealth by investing in a broad range of assets, shielding returns from tax inside a Stocks and Shares ISA or building retirement savings through a SIPP. The platform is FCA regulated, giving UK users strong consumer protections, though all investments carry risk and the value of your portfolio can fall as well as rise. It suits everyone from beginners to experienced investors, though its percentage-based fees may be less competitive for very large portfolios.",
    "interactive investor": "Interactive investor is a major UK investment platform operating on a flat monthly subscription model rather than percentage-based fees, making it particularly cost-effective for investors holding larger portfolios. You can trade UK and international shares, funds, investment trusts, and ETFs, and hold them within an ISA or SIPP to shelter gains and income from tax. The platform is FCA regulated, and as with all investing, your capital is at risk. It suits confident, engaged investors who trade regularly or hold significant sums, as the flat-fee structure becomes increasingly competitive the more you have invested compared with percentage-charging rivals.",
    "Dodl by AJ Bell": "Dodl is a streamlined, low-cost investing app built by established UK broker AJ Bell, designed to make investing more accessible for everyday UK users. You can invest in a curated selection of funds and individual shares commission-free, holding them within a Stocks and Shares ISA or pension wrapper to manage your tax position efficiently. The platform is FCA regulated and backed by the financial strength of AJ Bell, offering reassurance alongside simplicity. It suits those who want a no-fuss, affordable entry point into investing without the complexity of a full brokerage platform, though the fund and share selection is more limited than AJ Bell's main service. Capital is at risk.",
    "Wombat": "Wombat is a UK micro-investing app aimed at those who want to start building an investment portfolio with small, regular amounts. You can invest in themed funds aligned to topics such as technology or sustainability, as well as buy fractional shares in well-known companies, meaning you don't need large sums to get started. Investments can be held within a Stocks and Shares ISA, helping UK users shelter potential returns from income tax and capital gains tax. The app is FCA regulated and suits younger investors or complete beginners looking for an approachable introduction to investing, though as with all investing, the value of your holdings can go down as well as up.",
    "Chip": "Chip is a UK savings and investing app that uses automation to help you build financial resilience without constant manual effort. It offers a range of accounts including easy-access savings, cash ISAs, and investment options, with the app able to analyse your spending and set money aside automatically. Interest rates on savings accounts are competitive, though rates vary and are not guaranteed to remain fixed. UK users should note that savings held in FCA-regulated deposit accounts may be protected by the Financial Services Compensation Scheme up to applicable limits, while investment accounts carry capital risk. Chip suits people who struggle to save consistently and want technology to take the effort out of growing their money.",
    "CrowdProperty": "CrowdProperty is a UK peer-to-peer property lending platform that allows individual investors to earn interest by lending money directly to vetted residential and commercial property developers and borrowers. Each loan is secured against UK property assets, providing a layer of security, though this does not eliminate risk and your capital is at risk if borrowers default or security values fall. The platform is FCA regulated and targets investors seeking returns above typical savings rates without investing directly in the stock market. It suits UK investors who understand lending risk and want property-backed exposure in their portfolio, and any interest earned must be declared to HMRC as income for self-assessment purposes.",
    "Assetz Capital": "Assetz Capital is a UK peer-to-peer lending platform that connects individual investors with British small and medium-sized businesses and property developers seeking funding. Investors earn interest on loans, with rates varying depending on the account type and the risk profile of underlying borrowers. Loans are typically asset-backed, offering some security, but this does not guarantee your capital and losses are possible if borrowers default. The platform is FCA regulated and suits UK investors comfortable with the risks of alternative finance who are looking for returns that may exceed those available from traditional savings products. Interest income must be reported to HMRC, and you should consider diversifying across multiple loans to spread risk.",
    "Kuflink": "Kuflink is an FCA-regulated UK peer-to-peer lending platform specialising in property-backed loans, allowing investors to earn monthly interest by funding loans secured against UK real estate. You can invest manually in individual loan parts or use the auto-invest feature to spread funds automatically across multiple loans, and an Innovative Finance ISA wrapper is available to shelter interest from income tax. While loans are secured against property, capital is at risk and security values may not fully cover losses in the event of borrower default. Kuflink suits UK investors seeking regular monthly income with a property-secured element to their alternative finance portfolio, though interest earned outside an ISA must be declared to HMRC.",
    "Gumtree": "Gumtree is the UK's best-known free classifieds website, allowing private sellers to list almost anything — from furniture and electronics to cars and clothing — at no charge. You earn by agreeing a price with a local buyer, then arranging collection or delivery directly. Because transactions are typically cash-in-hand or bank transfer between individuals, there are no platform fees eating into your proceeds. It suits anyone wanting a straightforward way to declutter or sell locally without opening a shop. UK sellers should be aware that regular selling for profit may be considered a trading activity by HMRC, making accurate record-keeping advisable.",
    "Preloved": "Preloved is a long-established UK classifieds marketplace dedicated to second-hand goods, covering everything from fashion and furniture to pets and property. Private sellers can list items for free, while a low-cost premium membership unlocks additional features such as more photos and highlighted listings. You earn by connecting with local or nationwide buyers and agreeing payment and collection directly. The platform suits casual declutterers and regular resellers alike. As with any online selling activity, UK users who sell frequently for profit should consider whether their earnings need to be declared to HMRC as trading income.",
    "Vestiaire Collective": "Vestiaire Collective is a global marketplace for pre-loved luxury and designer fashion, widely used by UK buyers and sellers seeking authenticated second-hand pieces. You earn by listing items such as designer handbags, clothing and accessories, with the platform charging a commission on each completed sale. Vestiaire offers an optional authentication service, which can increase buyer confidence and help command higher prices. It suits UK sellers with quality designer pieces who want access to an international audience beyond domestic marketplaces. Sellers should factor commission and any postage costs into their pricing, and frequent sellers may need to report earnings to HMRC.",
    "Music Magpie": "Music Magpie is a UK-based resale service that allows you to sell old smartphones, tablets, tech, CDs, DVDs, books and video games quickly and easily. You enter the barcode or device details on their website or app to receive an instant fixed quote, then post your items free of charge using their prepaid label. Payment is made once the goods are received and inspected. It suits people who want a hassle-free, no-negotiation way to clear unwanted tech and media without the effort of listing items individually. Payouts are straightforward, though quotes reflect resale value, so prices may be lower than private sale alternatives.",
    "Folksy": "Folksy is a UK-based online marketplace specifically designed for independent makers, crafters and artists selling handmade or designed-in-Britain goods. You earn by listing original creations — such as jewellery, ceramics, prints and textiles — to a community of buyers who actively seek to support British makers. Folksy charges a small listing fee per item and takes a commission on each sale, with a paid Plus plan available to remove listing fees for higher-volume sellers. It suits UK creative professionals and hobbyists alike. As earnings from craft sales count as self-employment income, sellers should register with HMRC and keep records for Self Assessment purposes.",
    "OnBuy": "OnBuy is a fast-growing UK-based online marketplace where independent sellers can list new or used products to millions of British shoppers as an alternative to larger international platforms. Sellers pay a monthly subscription and a commission on completed sales, with rates varying by category. Listings reach a growing domestic audience actively looking to support UK-based retail. It suits small businesses, sole traders and established retailers wanting additional sales channels. Because selling on OnBuy is a business activity, income must be declared to HMRC, and sellers operating as sole traders should ensure they are registered for Self Assessment and, if applicable, VAT.",
    "Shpock": "Shpock is a mobile-first marketplace app popular in the UK for buying and selling second-hand items locally. You earn by photographing items on your phone, creating a quick listing and agreeing a sale price with a nearby buyer, with collection or local delivery typically arranged between parties. Listing is free for private sellers, though optional promoted features are available to boost visibility. It suits anyone wanting a simple, app-based way to sell unwanted household goods, clothing or electronics without the formality of a full online shop. UK sellers who use the platform regularly for profit should consider whether their activity requires reporting to HMRC.",
    "PopulusLive": "PopulusLive is a UK survey panel operated by Populus, a well-established British polling and research company whose work regularly informs public debate and media coverage. Because the panel focuses on quality research rather than volume, members receive fewer survey invitations than on many rival sites, but those surveys tend to offer higher rewards per completion. You earn real money rather than points, which is credited to your account and withdrawn once you reach the payment threshold. PopulusLive suits UK adults who prefer occasional, more meaningful surveys over a constant drip of low-value tasks. Earnings are considered miscellaneous income, so keep records for HMRC if amounts become significant.",
    "20Cogs": "20Cogs is a UK get-paid-to site built around a structured challenge of completing a set number of free or trial offers, surveys, and sign-ups, with a lump-sum cashout available once all cogs are finished. Rather than earning small amounts indefinitely, you work through a defined list, making the potential payout feel more tangible than on traditional survey panels. Some cogs require a free registration, while others may involve a short free trial, so it is important to track cancellation dates carefully to avoid unwanted charges. It suits methodical UK users who are comfortable managing multiple sign-ups. Any earnings should be noted for HMRC self-assessment purposes.",
    "OnePoll": "OnePoll is a long-running UK survey panel that supplies opinion data to newspapers, broadcasters, and brands, so your responses may well end up quoted in news stories. Surveys are typically short and straightforward, and unusually for the industry, rewards are paid in real money rather than a points-based currency, making your balance easy to understand. Payouts are made via PayPal once you reach the cashout threshold. The panel suits UK adults looking for a simple, low-effort way to earn a modest side income in spare moments. As with all survey income, earnings count as taxable miscellaneous income, so keep a record for HMRC if they accumulate.",
    "Triaba": "Triaba is an international survey panel with a dedicated UK-facing site that connects British consumers with market research companies seeking targeted feedback. Members are paid a cash amount for each survey they complete, with rewards accumulated in their account and withdrawn to PayPal once the minimum threshold is reached. Survey frequency and length vary depending on your demographic profile, and not every invitation will result in a full survey if you do not qualify at the screening stage. Triaba suits UK adults who already use PayPal and want a straightforward cash-based panel to run alongside others. Survey income should be declared to HMRC if it contributes meaningfully to your overall earnings.",
    "The OpinionPanel": "The OpinionPanel, sometimes known as OpinionPanel Community, is a UK research platform specifically designed for students and young people aged roughly 16 to 30, making it one of the few panels that actively targets this demographic. Members are invited to share their views on topics relevant to education, lifestyle, and consumer habits through surveys, discussions, and research studies, earning cash payments or high-street vouchers in return. It is particularly well suited to university students looking to earn small amounts around their studies without committing to fixed hours. Participants should be aware that survey and voucher income may still need to be reported to HMRC depending on their individual tax circumstances.",
    "Reed.co.uk": "Reed.co.uk is one of the UK's largest and longest-established job boards, listing thousands of remote and flexible vacancies across virtually every sector, from administration and finance to technology and healthcare. To earn through Reed, you create a free account, upload your CV, and apply directly to remote roles posted by UK employers. It suits anyone seeking employed or contract remote work, whether full-time, part-time, or temporary. Because roles are standard employment or self-employed contracts, any income is subject to PAYE tax or self-assessment with HMRC as appropriate. Reed itself does not pay you; it simply connects you with hiring employers.",
    "CV-Library": "CV-Library is one of the UK's most widely used job boards, with a dedicated remote-working category that makes it straightforward to find home-based and flexible roles across a broad range of industries and salary levels. You register for free, upload your CV, and can either apply for vacancies directly or make your profile searchable so that recruiters and employers can approach you. It suits jobseekers at any career stage who want employed or contract remote work. Income is paid by the hiring employer and must be declared to HMRC under PAYE or self-assessment depending on your employment status. CV-Library charges no fee to candidates.",
    "Totaljobs": "Totaljobs is a leading UK recruitment platform that lists a wide range of remote, hybrid, and home-based vacancies across industries including marketing, IT, customer service, and finance. You create a free candidate account, upload your CV, and search or set up job alerts specifically for remote roles. Employers and recruitment agencies post positions ranging from entry-level to senior management, so it suits a broad audience of UK jobseekers. Earnings depend entirely on the role and employer you secure; Totaljobs itself is purely a matching platform. All income received from any position found through Totaljobs remains subject to standard UK tax obligations via PAYE or self-assessment.",
    "Otta": "Otta, now part of the Welcome to the Jungle platform, is a curated job discovery service focused primarily on technology, start-up, and growth-company roles across the UK and Europe, with strong remote-working options. Unlike traditional job boards, it personalises the roles shown to you based on your skills, experience, and preferences, helping you cut through irrelevant listings. It suits tech professionals, product managers, designers, engineers, and marketers seeking remote or hybrid positions with ambitious companies. Registration and job applications are free for candidates. Any salary or contract income secured through the platform is paid by the employer and must be declared to HMRC in the usual way.",
    "Buy Me a Coffee": "Buy Me a Coffee is a straightforward platform that allows UK creators to receive one-off tips, set up monthly memberships, and sell digital products directly to their audience. Supporters pay in a few clicks without needing an account, making it low-friction for fans to show appreciation. It suits bloggers, artists, podcasters, writers, and anyone building an online following who wants a simple income stream alongside their main content. Earnings are not guaranteed and will vary considerably depending on audience size and engagement. UK creators should register as self-employed with HMRC and declare all income received through the platform for tax purposes.",
    "Fanvue": "Fanvue is a UK-founded subscription platform that enables creators to monetise their content through monthly fan subscriptions, tips, pay-per-view messages, and exclusive posts. Designed as a creator-first alternative to similar platforms, it offers competitive revenue splits and built-in promotional tools to help grow your audience. It suits influencers, fitness coaches, adult content creators, and anyone with a dedicated following looking for recurring income. Earnings vary widely depending on your niche and audience engagement, and nothing is guaranteed. UK creators should register as self-employed with HMRC, declare all earnings, and be aware of relevant content regulations when publishing on the platform."
}
